"""You can see the description of the task using the code specified in the title on leetcode.
This program has O(n) complexity by time and O(n) by space.

I gave it a lot of thought, but I only managed to solve it with some hints from Claude. 
The key insight is to view our path as a closed loop; 
in this model, our operations either shift the starting point (the index from which we begin counting) or change the direction in which we count. 
Essentially, none of our operations can alter the structure of the loop itself. 
The problem then boils down to finding a specific starting point from which we can traverse the entire array 
and end up with a sorted sequence - while also determining the minimum number of steps required to reach that point.

Then we look for 0 and determine whether it is possible to traverse the array starting from it in sorted order.
After that, we calculate the minimum number of rotation and shift operations required to achieve this, in order to find the overall minimum.
"""

class Solution:
    def minOperations(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:
        size = len(nums)
        start = nums.index(0)
        best = -1
        if all(nums[(start + j) % size] == j for j in range(size)):
            best = min(start, size - start + 2)
        if all(nums[(start - j) % size] == j for j in range(size)):
            p = (size - 1 - start) % size
            c = min(p, size - p) + 1
            best = c if best < 0 else min(best, c)
        return best
